    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e${{cost_ps}} a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e (at everyday cost) for a layered plate with real pork shoulder, beans, rice and cheese. Rice and beans are the cheapest foundation in cooking, and this dish knows it.",
    "shop_smart":  [
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eTomatillos are cheaper than they look.\u003c/strong\u003e Buy them by the pound with the husks on, peel and rinse the sticky coating off, and you have the tang that makes this salsa work.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eArbol chiles are sold by the bag for pocket change.\u003c/strong\u003e A few of them bring real heat to a big pot of salsa, and the bag will outlast several batches.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eBuy the shredded Mexican cheese blend on sale and freeze it.\u003c/strong\u003e Shredded cheese freezes fine and thaws ready to use, so there is no reason to pay the regular price.",
                       "\u003cstrong\u003eKeep the three components separate until you plate.\u003c/strong\u003e Rice, beans and pork stored apart reheat much better than one stirred-together mess, and it costs nothing to do it right."
                   ],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Cube the pork shoulder, season with salt and black pepper, and brown it in a heavy pot with a little olive oil, then add the bay leaves and enough water to nearly cover and simmer 90 minutes until tender.",
                    "While the pork cooks, blend the diced tomatoes, the husked tomatillos, the jalapeno, the arbol chiles, half the onion and the garlic into a rough salsa.",
                    "Cook the remaining diced onion in the pot, pour in the blended salsa, and let it fry and reduce for 15 minutes until it darkens and thickens.",
                    "Return the tender pork to the salsa and simmer another 20 minutes so the meat takes it on.",
                    "Cook the rice per the package, and warm the kidney beans with a little of their liquid so they stay soupy rather than dry.",
                    "Layer each portion rice first, then beans, then the saucy pork, and finish with the shredded Mexican cheese blend and chopped cilantro."
                ],
